NAME

ulibGetFileData - Returns information about a file catalogue entry


SYNOPSIS

long ulibGetFileData(struct FileCat *f, long AccessType, long *DataSize)
                          a0                d0                 a1


DESCRIPTION

This function returns information about the file catalogue entry pointed to
by <f>.

The <AccessType> parameter may be any one of the #define'd XFD_ codes in
xenolink.h (such as XFD_NAME, XFD_UPLOADER, XFD_DESCRIPTION, for example).

<DataSize> is valid only for certain string variables:

   XFD_NAME         File name
   XFD_UPLOADER     Name/alias of uploader

If <DataSize> is not NULL, it should point to a long integer into which the
maximum allowable length of the string (including null terminator) will be
placed (e.g. FILENAME_LENGTH or USERNAME_LENGTH).

Note that although FILENAME_LENGTH is the maximum allowable length of the
name of a file catalogue entry (including the null terminator), the Sysop
may have set up his system such that filenames may not exceed a certain
length, in particular sections.

It is up to you to ensure that if you change a file's name, or add a new file
to the catalogue, that you do not create too long a name; doing so will not
damage the system in any way, but will:

   1. Cause the file catalogue listing to look untidy.
   2. Annoy the Sysop.
   3. Possibly prevent users of PC compatible computers from downloading the
      file, if the filename does not fit into the PC's 8.3 filename format
      (assuming the PC user is using a primitive terminal).

To obtain the maximum length of a filename in a particular section, use the
ulibGetSystemData(XSD_FILE_MAX_NAME_LEN, SectionNumber) function call.

The Sysop may have set an additional restriction for filenames; Xenolink
allows sections to be configured to use the PC's 8.3 filename format.  To
check for the presence of this flag in a file section, use the
ulibGetSystemData(XSD_FILE_MSDOS_FILENAMES, SectionNumber) function call.

If the Sysop has indeed configured filenames for the PC's 8.3 format, you
must ensure that your filename follows this format.

Using the XFD_DESCRIPTION parameter will return a pointer to the file's
description if <f->Description> is not NULL.  Xenolink will NOT load the
file's description from disk; ulibGetFileData() only operates on data already
in memory.


NOTES

Usage of <DataSize> is unnecessary and not recommended, since the maximum
string lengths of file data strings is provided in xenolink.h.  These numbers
are highly unlikely to change!


RETURNS

This function returns the requested information as either:

   1. A signed long integer (for integral file fields)
      (Note that all integral return values are promoted to signed long
      integers)

   2. A pointer to a string (for string variables such as FileName, Uploader)

   3. A pointer to a (struct TimeDate) (for XFD_DATE_UPLOADED,
      XFD_LAST_DOWNLOADED).


SEE ALSO

